p=Path('src/biology/learning/organelles-compartmentalization/lesson.json')
l=json.loads(p.read_text())
blocks=[
[
('A function needs a structure','A ribosome links amino acids into a polypeptide; a vesicle encloses cargo; the plasma membrane controls exchange at the cell boundary. Each is a discrete subunit adapted to a function. For this course, nuclei, vesicles, ribosomes and the plasma membrane are organelles. The cell wall, cytoskeleton and cytoplasm are not classified as organelles in the IB understanding. A membrane is therefore not the defining test: ribosomes are not membrane-bound. Prokaryotes have ribosomes and a plasma membrane, even though they lack a membrane-enclosed nucleus.'),
('Open the cell without destroying every organelle','Seeing a structure does not tell us all its functions. Researchers can gently disrupt cells in a suitable medium to make a homogenate while retaining many intact organelles. Conditions matter: if organelles rupture, their contents may appear in the wrong fraction. The preparation is then centrifuged. A pellet collects sedimented material, while the supernatant contains material still suspended. The supernatant can be spun again at a higher force or for longer to obtain another enriched fraction.'),
('Separate, then test the fractions','In differential centrifugation, nuclei generally sediment before smaller organelles; mitochondria can be enriched in a later pellet, and ribosomes require higher forces or longer times. Sedimentation depends on particle size, density and shape, as well as the medium and spinning conditions. This differs from equilibrium density-gradient separation, which sorts particles by buoyant density. Fractions are enriched mixtures, not automatically pure preparations. Testing more than one marker helps distinguish the activity of an organelle from contamination.'),
('Why the technique changed the question','The ultracentrifuge made much finer separation possible. Scientists could ask whether a biochemical activity followed a particular organelle fraction, then compare results across preparations. This links a structure to evidence of function, rather than relying on appearance alone. A high mitochondrial marker in a fraction supports mitochondrial enrichment. It does not show that every other organelle is absent or that all mitochondria remain functional. In the investigation, use competing markers to decide which fraction deserves further study.')],
[
('One gene, two different processes','Transcription copies information from a DNA template into RNA. Translation uses the sequence of an mRNA to assemble a polypeptide at a ribosome. For nuclear genes in a eukaryotic cell, transcription occurs inside the nucleus while translation occurs at ribosomes in the cytoplasm. The nuclear envelope separates those locations. DNA does not have to leave the nucleus for its information to be used; an RNA copy can travel out.'),
('Prepare the message before it is read','Many eukaryotic protein-coding genes contain introns between exons. A primary transcript includes both. Splicing removes introns and joins exons to produce a continuous message. Other processing steps also contribute to making a mature mRNA. Separation means processing can occur before the message encounters cytoplasmic ribosomes. Alternative splicing can produce different mature messages from one gene. Not every eukaryotic gene contains introns, so splicing is an example of processing rather than a compulsory step for every transcript.'),
('Export is an organized exchange','Mature mRNA leaves through a nuclear pore as part of an RNA–protein complex. Nuclear proteins made in the cytoplasm can enter through pores. This controlled exchange lets the cell retain its genome and RNA-processing machinery while supplying the cytoplasm with messages for translation. A pore is a regulated protein complex spanning the envelope; it is not a tear through which chromosomes normally escape. Removing introns is done by RNA-processing machinery, not by the translating ribosome.'),
('Compare with a prokaryotic cell','Without a nuclear envelope separating DNA from ribosomes, a prokaryotic mRNA can meet ribosomes while transcription is still under way. This coupling contrasts with the opportunity for nuclear processing before cytoplasmic translation in eukaryotes. Keep the comparison focused on location and timing. It does not justify claiming that no prokaryotic RNA is ever processed or that introns never occur in prokaryotes. In the investigation, track a message containing an intron and explain what the boundary makes possible.')],
[
('Create a local chemical environment','A membrane-bounded compartment can hold enzymes and metabolites together instead of diluting them throughout the cytosol. Concentration affects how often reactants encounter suitable enzymes. Membrane transport also helps establish local conditions, including pH. These benefits require controlled exchange: substrates must enter and products must leave. A compartment is a regulated reaction space, not a permanently sealed container.'),
('Keep incompatible processes apart','Lysosomes contain hydrolytic enzymes that break down biological material. Proton pumping maintains an acidic lumen where many of these acid hydrolases work effectively. The surrounding cytosol is closer to neutral. The membrane both confines the enzymes and separates their favourable reaction conditions from other cellular processes. Acidity alone is not a guarantee against damage: lysosomal rupture can harm a cell, even though many acid hydrolases are less active at cytosolic pH.'),
('Follow a bacterium inside a phagocyte','A phagocytic white blood cell recognizes and engulfs a particle, enclosing it in a phagocytic vacuole, or phagosome, derived from the plasma membrane. Fusion with lysosomes brings digestive machinery to the enclosed material, producing a phagolysosome. Acidification and enzymes support breakdown within this bounded space. The bacterium is inside the cell but still separated from the cytosol by a membrane. Intracellular location does not imply direct contact with the cytosol.'),
('Distinguish containment from chemistry','Suppose lysosome fusion occurs normally, but a treatment inhibits acidification. The compartment can still contain the particle and concentrate enzymes, while providing less favourable conditions for acid hydrolases. Predicting reduced digestion therefore connects a membrane-associated mechanism to a functional outcome. Conversely, putting digestive enzymes throughout the cytosol would remove the controlled reaction space. Use these different interventions to explain why concentration, chemical conditions and separation each matter.')],
[
('Locate the membranes and spaces','A mitochondrion has an outer membrane and an inner membrane. The inner membrane encloses the matrix and folds into cristae; those folds are continuous with the inner boundary membrane. The intermembrane space lies outside the inner membrane and is continuous with the spaces within cristae. These are different aqueous compartments. A diagram is a simplified section, not a map of every fold or a measurement of real distances.'),
('Link folds to membrane machinery','Respiratory electron-transfer complexes and ATP synthase occupy the inner membrane. Cristae provide a large membrane surface for this machinery without requiring a proportionate increase in organelle volume. Electron transfer releases energy used to pump H+ from the matrix side toward the intermembrane space. Oxygen is the terminal electron acceptor in the respiratory chain. The folds do not simply provide extra storage space for oxygen.'),
('Use a gradient, not just a membrane','Proton accumulation produces an electrochemical difference across the inner membrane. H+ can return toward the matrix through ATP synthase, coupling that flow to ATP formation from ADP and phosphate. A small intermembrane volume allows a given net proton transfer to change concentration substantially. However, real gradients also depend on buffering, electrical potential, pumping and leak pathways. The concentration model isolates geometry; it cannot calculate ATP yield or rate.'),
('Coordinate matrix reactions with the membrane','The matrix contains enzymes and substrates for the Krebs cycle and other reactions supplying electron carriers to respiration. Compartmentalization keeps these reactions organized near the inner-membrane machinery that uses electrons from reduced carriers. Glycolysis occurs in the cytosol, so not every stage of glucose breakdown occurs in mitochondria. Explain each required adaptation as a cause and consequence: cristae increase working area; distinct spaces permit a gradient; a small receiving volume favours concentration change; the matrix contains relevant enzymes and substrates.')],
[
('Separate envelope from internal membranes','A chloroplast is enclosed by an outer and inner envelope membrane. Inside, thylakoid membranes form a separate system surrounding the thylakoid lumen. Thylakoids are organized into grana connected by stromal lamellae. The stroma is the fluid region surrounding that membrane system. Do not draw mature thylakoids as folds continuous with the inner envelope: unlike mitochondrial cristae, they form a distinct internal membrane system.'),
('Give light capture a large working surface','Photosystems containing chlorophyll and associated proteins are embedded in thylakoid membranes. Their large combined membrane area supports light capture and electron transfer. Light supplies energy that ultimately supports formation of ATP and reducing power. It is the arrangement of pigments and proteins within membrane machinery that matters; a granum is not a bag of free chlorophyll dissolved in the lumen.'),
('Build up protons inside the thylakoid','During light-dependent reactions, proton transfer and water oxidation help H+ accumulate in the thylakoid lumen. Its small fluid volume favours a substantial concentration change for a given net proton accumulation. H+ returns through ATP synthase from lumen toward stroma. The resulting ATP is made on the stromal side. This direction differs in compartment names from mitochondrial proton return, even though both use an electrochemical gradient across a membrane.'),
('Connect the stroma to carbon fixation','The stroma contains enzymes and substrates of the Calvin cycle. ATP and reducing power from the light-dependent reactions support carbon fixation and carbohydrate production there. This is cooperation between compartments: the thylakoid membrane organizes light-driven reactions, while the surrounding stroma supports the carbon-fixation pathway. Calling the Calvin cycle light-independent does not mean that it normally operates independently of the products and regulation associated with light. Use location and direction to compare chloroplasts with mitochondria.')],
[
('Two membranes define a controlled compartment','The nuclear envelope consists of inner and outer membranes separated by a perinuclear space. Each membrane is a lipid bilayer; a double membrane does not mean the two leaflets of one bilayer. The outer membrane is continuous with the endoplasmic reticulum, and the perinuclear space is continuous with the ER lumen. The inner membrane faces nucleoplasm containing chromatin. In animal cells, an underlying nuclear lamina helps support nuclear organization.'),
('A boundary must still communicate','Nuclear pore complexes span openings where the membranes meet and provide selective exchange between nucleus and cytoplasm. RNA-containing particles can leave; proteins required in the nucleus can enter. Without functional pores, keeping the genome separate would also isolate it from supplies and prevent appropriate RNA export. The double boundary and regulated transport work together. Do not confuse the perinuclear space with the channel through a nuclear pore.'),
('Reorganize the boundary during division','For the open mitosis and meiosis described in the syllabus, the nuclear envelope breaks down, including fragmentation into vesicular membrane material, so spindle microtubules can access chromosomes. Membrane components also redistribute through the ER in many animal cells; a picture of identical detached vesicles is a simplified course model. The envelope later reforms around separated chromosome sets, restoring nuclear compartments. DNA is segregated during this process, not translated.'),
('Keep the biological qualification','The requirement concerns the functional benefit of opening the nuclear compartment for chromosome segregation. Some eukaryotes undergo closed division, retaining a nuclear envelope. That wider exception should not erase the required explanation of open mitosis and meiosis. In the investigation, compare blocked pores during interphase with failed envelope breakdown during division. The former interrupts exchange; the latter obstructs spindle access. Naming the structure is not enough—identify when it must function and why.')],
[
('The same translation machinery, different destinations','A ribosome consists of rRNA and proteins organized into subunits. It reads mRNA and joins amino acids into a polypeptide. Free and ER-bound ribosomes are not permanently different ribosome species. Many ribosomes can translate one mRNA simultaneously, forming a polysome and producing multiple polypeptide copies. A free ribosome commonly makes a protein used in the cytosol, such as a glycolysis enzyme.'),
('Let the nascent protein specify the route','Translation starts on a ribosome exposed to cytosol. For many secretory and endomembrane proteins, a targeting signal in the growing chain directs the translating complex to the rough ER. The ribosome attaches on the cytosolic face; the chain enters the ER lumen or is inserted into its membrane as synthesis continues. The ribosome itself does not enter the lumen. Targeting follows information associated with the cargo rather than a conscious choice by the ribosome.'),
('Use the ER as an entry and processing site','The rough ER is an interconnected membrane system surrounding a lumen. Attached ribosomes give it its rough appearance. Proteins entering the lumen can fold and undergo initial processing before transport toward the Golgi. Membrane proteins can remain embedded with a defined orientation. Transport vesicles keep lumenal cargo separated from cytosol while carrying it between compartments. Synthesis, folding and transport are distinct processes performed by cooperating structures.'),
('Avoid the export-only shortcut','A pancreatic digestive enzyme destined for secretion enters the ER route; a cytosolic enzyme commonly does not. A lysosomal enzyme also enters the endomembrane route yet functions inside the cell. Thus ER-bound does not mean every product must leave the cell. Conversely, many proteins synthesized on free ribosomes are later imported into other organelles. The useful course contrast is cytosolic retention versus entry into the secretory or endomembrane pathway, with these destinations kept in view.')],
[
('Recognize a polarized processing stack','The Golgi apparatus comprises flattened membrane-bounded sacs called cisternae. Its receiving, or cis, region accepts cargo arriving from the ER; its trans region sorts cargo for onward delivery. This polarity refers to the flow of cargo and the organization of processing functions. A Golgi stack is not a row of ribosomes, and the word processing does not mean that it reads mRNA to assemble a new polypeptide.'),
('Modify the product after synthesis','As cargo passes through Golgi compartments, enzymes can modify it. Examples include addition, removal or rearrangement of carbohydrate groups on glycoproteins; some proteins undergo other modifications. Different compartments have different enzyme complements, allowing a sequence of processing steps. The particular modifications depend on the protein. Do not infer that every protein receives exactly the same sugar chain or that Golgi processing is required for every protein made in the cell.'),
('Sort cargo before dispatch','Sorting information helps direct processed proteins toward destinations such as lysosomes, the plasma membrane or secretion outside the cell. A secretory protein can be packaged into a vesicle, transported to the cell surface and released when the vesicle membrane fuses with the plasma membrane. The vesicle lumen becomes continuous with the extracellular space at exocytosis. This explains how a protein can cross the cell boundary without first being released into cytosol.'),
('Infer a route from changing evidence','A pulse–chase experiment can label newly synthesized cargo and follow its location over time. If label first peaks in ER, later in Golgi and later in secretory vesicles, the order supports an ER-to-Golgi-to-vesicle route. It does not alone reveal every molecular transport mechanism or prove which chemical changes occurred. Real Golgi traffic involves dynamic cisternae and carriers; a simple directional diagram is a functional route, not proof that all stacks are static or all traffic is one-way.')],
[
('Preserve the sides of a membrane','A vesicle encloses a small volume of fluid and selected cargo within a membrane. During endocytosis, material outside the cell becomes enclosed in a lumen; it does not automatically enter cytosol. The membrane surface that originally faced cytosol continues to face cytosol after budding. This preserved topology helps explain why a receptor can bind external cargo while recruiting transport machinery through its cytosolic portion.'),
('Assemble a coat where cargo is collected','In clathrin-mediated endocytosis, adaptor proteins connect selected membrane cargo or receptors with clathrin on the cytosolic face. Clathrin assembles into a curved lattice that helps stabilize a coated pit as the membrane invaginates. It is outside the developing vesicle, not floating around the cargo inside. Coat formation organizes a bud; additional machinery is needed for membrane constriction and scission.'),
('Bud, uncoat and deliver','Scission separates a coated vesicle from the plasma membrane. The coat is then removed so cargo can proceed through downstream sorting and fusion events. Clathrin and adaptors can be reused. Delivery is selective: a vesicle must interact with appropriate targeting and fusion machinery rather than fusing randomly with any organelle. Clathrin is an example required by the syllabus, not the coat used by every vesicle in a cell.'),
('Compare uptake with release','Receptor-mediated uptake can concentrate a particular cargo in endocytic vesicles. Exocytosis works in the opposite topological direction: a vesicle membrane fuses with the plasma membrane and lumenal cargo is released outside. Neurotransmitter release from a neuron is a biological example of exocytosis; recycling of membrane components can involve endocytosis. The two processes connect intracellular compartments with the cell boundary without making the lipid bilayer freely permeable to large cargo.')]
]
scaffolds=[
'A cell contains both membrane-bounded spaces and non-membrane structures. A biochemical marker is an activity associated with a structure; a high marker signal is evidence of enrichment, not proof of purity.',
'DNA stores information. Transcription makes RNA from DNA; translation makes a polypeptide from mRNA. Exons are retained in the mature message; introns can be removed during processing.',
'An enzyme catalyses a reaction and has conditions under which it works well. A metabolite is a substance used or produced in metabolism. A membrane can separate two aqueous environments.',
'ATP couples energy-releasing processes to cellular work. An electrochemical gradient includes both concentration and electrical differences. A membrane protein can move ions across an otherwise restrictive bilayer.',
'Photosynthesis uses light energy to support carbon fixation. The thylakoid lumen is inside the thylakoid; stroma surrounds it. ATP synthase uses proton flow across a membrane.',
'Each membrane is one lipid bilayer. A double membrane means two bilayers. During cell division a spindle separates chromosomes; nuclear pores normally regulate RNA and protein exchange.',
'A ribosome translates mRNA into a polypeptide. Cytosol is fluid outside membrane-bounded organelles. ER lumen means the space enclosed by the ER membrane.',
'Translation first makes a polypeptide. A cisterna is a flattened membrane-bounded sac. A secretory vesicle carries material toward release at the cell surface.',
'A lipid bilayer has an extracellular or lumenal face and a cytosolic face. A receptor can connect cargo binding on one side with protein recruitment on the other.'
]
pages=[[244,245],[246,247],[247,248],[248,249],[249,250,251],[251,252,253],[253,254],[254],[255]]
